// PARKED REPRO (probe 2026-07-04): a float-to-int cast of an OUT-OF-RANGE value
// DIVERGES native-vs-interp, and the semantics are UNDECIDED. Same class as the
// shift-amount-out-of-range divergence.
//
// `1e20 as i32` (1e20 does not fit i32):
//   native = 70 (n == 0): x86 cvttsd2si on out-of-range yields the i64 "integer
//            indefinite" 0x8000000000000000; truncated to i32 that is 0.
//   interp = 71 (n == -1): eval does `f.trunc() as i64` (Rust SATURATES to
//            i64::MAX = 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), truncated to i32 = -1.
// Both are garbage (the value is out of range), but they differ.
// AARCH64 DATA POINT (2026-07-09, probe exit 99): FCVTZS SATURATES (like
// Rust/interp's i64 saturation, unlike x86's integer-indefinite), so the
// divergence is now also NATIVE-vs-NATIVE across architectures -- the design
// decision must pick trap/saturate/obligation, not "match native".
// LEDGER NOTE (2026-07-18): the pending-divergence gate runs native on the
// HOST, so its entry documents the x86 pair (native 70 / interp 71); this
// header keeps aarch64's 99 as the cross-target face. The RULING has since
// landed (proof-or-policy, OWNER_QUESTIONS 10 / float ladder F4); building
// F4 retires this repro entirely.
//
// UNIFIED DESIGN QUESTION -- MOSTLY RESOLVED (2026-07-16): the SHIFT half
// landed as F8 (Exact obligation + Wrapping masked count + Trapping count
// trap -- all three rungs). The FLOAT-TO-INT half is PARTIALLY built (F4a):
// `in Wrapping` float casts are REJECTED (no modular reading of a float --
// fail/arithmetic/float_cast_wrapping_rejected), `in Saturating` is the
// defined clamp policy (NaN -> 0, OOR -> bounds; interp + aarch64 FCVTZS,
// pinned arch-gated in pass/arithmetic/float_to_int_saturating_exit), and
// the interp's `in Trapping` cast traps on NaN/OOR. THIS REPRO (the BARE
// Exact cast) still stands: retiring it needs (a) the Exact value
// obligation (float constant tracking in validation), and (b) the x86
// Saturating/Trapping cast fixup sequences (cvttsd2si's integer-indefinite
// -- built on the x86 host with its runtime oracle) plus the native
// Trapping cast guard on both ISAs. In-range casts agree + are correct.

boundary trait Console { machine exit_process(return_code: i32); }
data Main { console: Console; f: f64; }
machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    self.f = 100000000000000000000.0;   // 1e20, out of i32 range
    let n: i32 = self.f as i32;
    transition n == 0 {
        true  -> native_zero()
        false -> chk()
    }
    state chk(&mut self) {
        transition n == 0 - 1 {
            true  -> interp_neg_one()
            false -> weird()
        }
    }
    state native_zero(&mut self)     { self.console.exit_process(70); }
    state interp_neg_one(&mut self)  { self.console.exit_process(71); }
    state weird(&mut self)           { self.console.exit_process(99); }
}
